/* AbiLearn — Social Science MCQ Bank — Ch 18, 19 & 20 (150 Questions) */

// CH18_SOCIAL (Civics Ch2 — Federalism)
const SOCIAL_CH18 = [
  { q: 'Which of the following BEST defines federalism?', opts: ['A system where all power rests with the central government', 'A system where power is divided between a central authority and its constituent units by a written constitution', 'A system where states can override any central law', 'A system based on single-party rule'], ans: 1, exp: 'Federalism is a system of government in which power is constitutionally divided between a central (national) government and regional (state) governments. Neither level can unilaterally abolish the other, and the division is enforced by a written constitution.' },
  { q: 'In a unitary system of government, sub-units derive their powers from:', opts: ['The constitution directly', 'The central government at its discretion', 'An independent judiciary', 'International treaties'], ans: 1, exp: 'In a unitary system, there is only one level of government or sub-units exist subordinate to and at the pleasure of the central authority. Powers are delegated, not constitutionally guaranteed — unlike in a federal system.' },
  { q: 'The "coming together" federations such as USA, Switzerland, and Australia are characterised by:', opts: ['Powerful states joining to form a stronger central government while retaining identity', 'A strong centre dividing power downwards to states', 'States surrendering all sovereignty to the centre', 'Federations formed by military conquest'], ans: 0, exp: 'In "coming together" federations, previously independent states voluntarily pool their sovereignty to form a union for security and joint benefits, but retain considerable autonomy. USA, Switzerland, and Australia are classic examples.' },
  { q: 'India\'s federal model is described as a "holding together" federation primarily because:', opts: ['India was formed by independent states voluntarily joining', 'A large country decided to divide power among constituent units to accommodate diversity while keeping the union intact', 'India has equal power distribution between centre and states', 'India\'s states can secede freely'], ans: 1, exp: 'In "holding together" federations like India, Spain, and Belgium, a large country divides power to accommodate regional diversity. The central government tends to be stronger relative to states, unlike in "coming together" federations.' },
  { q: 'Under the Indian Constitution, which list contains subjects on which ONLY the Parliament can legislate?', opts: ['State List', 'Concurrent List', 'Residuary List', 'Union List'], ans: 3, exp: 'The Union List (List I, 7th Schedule) contains 100 subjects — including defence, foreign affairs, banking, currency — on which only Parliament can make laws. This reflects the supremacy of the centre on critical national matters.' },
  { q: 'The State List under the Indian Constitution originally contained how many subjects?', opts: ['52', '61', '66', '97'], ans: 2, exp: 'The State List (List II) originally contained 66 subjects on which state legislatures have exclusive power to legislate, including police, agriculture, public health, and land. (Parliament can legislate on these in certain extraordinary circumstances.)' },
  { q: 'On subjects in the Concurrent List, in case of a conflict between central and state law, which prevails?', opts: ['State law always prevails', 'The older law prevails regardless of source', 'Central law prevails, unless the state law received Presidential assent before the central law', 'Neither prevails; a court must decide arbitrarily'], ans: 2, exp: 'Article 254 states that in case of inconsistency between Parliament and state legislature laws on Concurrent List subjects, the central law prevails. However, if the state law was reserved for and received Presidential assent before the central law, the state law may prevail in that state.' },
  { q: 'Which constitutional provision deals with "Residuary Powers" in India?', opts: ['Article 245', 'Article 246', 'Article 248', 'Article 356'], ans: 2, exp: 'Article 248 vests residuary powers — on subjects not enumerated in any of the three lists — exclusively with Parliament. Entry 97 of the Union List reinforces this. Unlike the USA (where residuary powers go to states), in India they go to the centre.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is NOT a key feature that makes India a federal state?', opts: ['Constitutional division of powers between centre and states', 'Independent and powerful judiciary', 'Dual citizenship for central and state subjects', 'Bicameral Parliament with a Rajya Sabha representing states'], ans: 2, exp: 'India has single citizenship — all Indians are citizens of India, not of their respective states. This is unlike the USA where dual citizenship exists. The other options — constitutional division of powers, independent judiciary, and bicameral Parliament — are genuine federal features of India.' },
  { q: 'The Rajya Sabha reflects the federal principle in India because:', opts: ['It has equal representation of all states regardless of population', 'It represents the states and Union Territories in Parliament at the centre', 'It can veto any bill passed by the Lok Sabha', 'Its members are directly elected by citizens'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Rajya Sabha (Council of States) is the upper house of Parliament and represents the states and UTs at the national level, embodying the federal principle. However, unlike the US Senate, representation is not equal — larger states have more seats.' },
  { q: 'Which amendment of the Indian Constitution reorganised states largely on linguistic lines?', opts: ['7th Amendment, 1956', '13th Amendment, 1962', '18th Amendment, 1966', '36th Amendment, 1975'], ans: 0, exp: 'The 7th Constitutional Amendment (1956) reorganised states on linguistic lines following the States Reorganisation Act of 1956 and the Fazl Ali Commission report, creating a new map of India largely based on language.' },
  { q: 'The States Reorganisation Act of 1956 was based on the recommendations of which commission?', opts: ['Dhar Commission', 'JVP Committee', 'States Reorganisation Commission (Fazl Ali Commission)', 'Sarkaria Commission'], ans: 2, exp: 'The States Reorganisation Commission (1953), chaired by Justice Fazl Ali, recommended reorganising states primarily on linguistic basis. Its report led to the States Reorganisation Act, 1956, and the 7th Amendment which created new linguistic states.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following sets of states was created in November 2000?', opts: ['Telangana, Jharkhand, Uttarakhand', 'Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Uttarakhand', 'Chhattisgarh, Telangana, Sikkim', 'Jharkhand, Meghalaya, Mizoram'], ans: 1, exp: 'Three new states were carved out of existing states in November 2000: Chhattisgarh from Madhya Pradesh, Jharkhand from Bihar, and Uttarakhand (then Uttaranchal) from Uttar Pradesh, making India\'s state count 28 at the time.' },
  { q: 'Telangana was carved out of Andhra Pradesh to become India\'s 29th state in:', opts: ['2000', '2004', '2010', '2014'], ans: 3, exp: 'Telangana was created as a separate state from the existing state of Andhra Pradesh on 2 June 2014, becoming India\'s 29th state. The demand for a separate state was based on regional identity, economic concerns, and cultural distinctiveness of the Telangana region.' },
  { q: 'The concept of decentralisation in India refers to:', opts: ['Transfer of power from states to the centre', 'Transfer of some power from the union and state governments to local governments', 'Abolition of the district administrative system', 'Transfer of power from the judiciary to the executive'], ans: 1, exp: 'Decentralisation means transferring power downwards from higher levels of government (union and state) to local governments — panchayats and municipalities — enabling people to directly participate in governance at the grass-roots level.' },
  { q: 'The 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992 provided constitutional status to:', opts: ['Urban local bodies (municipalities)', 'Rural local bodies (Panchayati Raj institutions)', 'District courts', 'State Finance Commissions only'], ans: 1, exp: 'The 73rd Amendment (1992) gave constitutional status to Panchayati Raj institutions (PRIs) — rural local self-government. It added the 11th Schedule (29 subjects) and Part IX to the Constitution, mandating states to establish three-tier panchayats.' },
  { q: 'The 74th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992 is related to:', opts: ['Panchayati Raj in rural areas', 'Urban local self-government (Municipalities)', 'Parliamentary constituencies delimitation', 'State Finance Commissions for states'], ans: 1, exp: 'The 74th Amendment (1992) gave constitutional status to urban local bodies — municipalities, town area committees, notified area committees, etc. It added the 12th Schedule (18 subjects) and Part IX-A, mandating establishment of Wards Committees and reservation of seats.' },
  { q: 'The three tiers of rural local government (Panchayati Raj) from lowest to highest are:', opts: ['Gram Panchayat → Panchayat Samiti → Zila Parishad', 'Zila Parishad → Panchayat Samiti → Gram Panchayat', 'Nyaya Panchayat → Gram Sabha → Zila Parishad', 'Gram Sabha → Gram Panchayat → Panchayat Samiti'], ans: 0, exp: 'The three-tier Panchayati Raj system from bottom to top is: Gram Panchayat (village level) → Panchayat Samiti / Block Panchayat (intermediate/block level) → Zila Parishad (district level). This structure was prescribed by the 73rd Amendment.' },
  { q: 'A Gram Sabha in the Panchayati Raj system consists of:', opts: ['All elected members of the gram panchayat', 'All voters in the panchayat area (village-level)', 'Only the sarpanch and ward members', 'District-level officials and village representatives'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Gram Sabha is the general body of all adult voters registered in the electoral rolls of a gram panchayat area. It is the base of the Panchayati Raj system. It meets to approve annual budgets, audit accounts, and review development schemes.' },
  { q: 'The State Finance Commission (SFC), as mandated by the 73rd Amendment, is constituted to:', opts: ['Recommend distribution of financial resources between the state and local bodies', 'Audit accounts of state government expenditure', 'Recommend taxes to be levied by the Union government', 'Supervise election expenditure in panchayat elections'], ans: 0, exp: 'The 73rd Amendment (Article 243-I) requires every state to constitute a State Finance Commission every five years to review the financial position of panchayats and recommend principles governing distribution of taxes, grants, and other resources between the state and local bodies.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is a feature that makes India\'s federalism distinct from classic federal models?', opts: ['India has a written constitution', 'India has a strong independent judiciary', 'The Constitution provides for a stronger centre — certain subjects allow Parliament to legislate on State List items', 'India has a Rajya Sabha'], ans: 2, exp: 'India\'s federalism is asymmetric and centre-leaning. Under Articles 249, 250, 252, and 253, Parliament can legislate on State List subjects under certain conditions (national interest, emergency, state request, international treaty), making India\'s federalism more unitary in practice during crises.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes the Belgian federal model as discussed in the NCERT text?', opts: ['A coming-together federation of provinces with equal power', 'A system where communities have cultural autonomy alongside regional governments, managed through negotiation', 'A unitary state that delegated limited powers to regions', 'A model where the central government controls all cultural matters'], ans: 1, exp: 'Belgium\'s federal model is unique in that it combines territorial (regional) autonomy for Wallonia, Flanders, and Brussels with community-based cultural autonomy for French, Dutch, and German speakers. It is a result of negotiated power-sharing to prevent linguistic conflict.' },
  { q: 'Canada\'s federal model differs from Belgium\'s in that:', opts: ['Canada gives more power to provinces than Belgium gives to regions', 'Canada is a unitary state and Belgium is a federation', 'Canada has only two levels of government and Belgium has three', 'Canada\'s provinces have no fiscal autonomy'], ans: 0, exp: 'Canada is a "coming together" federation where provinces have significant autonomy including control over natural resources and social policy. Quebec retains distinct cultural-linguistic autonomy. Canada\'s model gives provinces relatively more power compared to Belgium\'s intricate community-regional balance.' },
  { q: 'In India, during a coalition government, the federal principle is:', opts: ['Weakened because the centre imposes its will on states', 'Often strengthened because regional parties in the coalition bargain for state autonomy', 'Irrelevant since coalition governments are a parliamentary phenomenon', 'Replaced by a unitary system temporarily'], ans: 1, exp: 'Coalition governments at the centre (post-1989 era) have included regional parties, which use their leverage to negotiate greater autonomy and resources for their states. This has in practice strengthened federalism — states have gained more say in policy-making.' },
  { q: 'Under the Indian Constitution, who has the authority to alter the boundaries of states or create new states?', opts: ['The state legislature concerned', 'Parliament alone, by a simple majority', 'Parliament by a special majority with ratification by states', 'The President by executive order'], ans: 1, exp: 'Under Article 3, Parliament can by a simple majority (not special majority) create new states, alter boundaries, or change names of existing states. However, the bill must be referred to the concerned state legislature(s) for views, though their consent is not mandatory.' },
  { q: 'What is a "nyaya panchayat" in the context of rural local government?', opts: ['A village-level body that manages primary schools', 'A judicial body at the panchayat level that settles minor local disputes', 'The executive committee of the gram panchayat', 'An NGO that monitors panchayat funds'], ans: 1, exp: 'A Nyaya Panchayat (or Gram Nyayalaya) is a judicial body at the village or cluster-of-village level designed to provide inexpensive and speedy justice for petty civil and criminal matters at the grassroots. Not all states have established them.' },
  { q: 'The 73rd Amendment mandated reservation of seats for women in panchayats at:', opts: ['20%', '25%', 'Not less than one-third (33%)', '50%'], ans: 2, exp: 'Article 243-D (as inserted by the 73rd Amendment) mandates reservation of not less than one-third of seats (and offices of chairpersons) in panchayats for women. Several states like Bihar, Rajasthan, and Uttarakhand have since enhanced this to 50%.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is NOT within the jurisdiction of local self-government bodies according to the 11th Schedule?', opts: ['Poverty alleviation programmes', 'Primary and secondary education', 'Maintenance of community assets', 'Defence preparedness'], ans: 3, exp: 'The 11th Schedule lists 29 subjects including agriculture, land improvement, minor irrigation, animal husbandry, fisheries, social forestry, primary education, cultural activities, and poverty alleviation — but NOT defence, which is a Union subject under Article 246.' },
  { q: 'The first State to implement the three-tier Panchayati Raj system after independence (before the 73rd Amendment) was:', opts: ['Kerala', 'Maharashtra', 'Rajasthan', 'Karnataka'], ans: 2, exp: 'Rajasthan was the first state to establish the three-tier Panchayati Raj system in 1959, inaugurated by Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru at Nagaur on 2 October 1959, acting on the recommendation of the Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957).' },
  { q: 'The Sarkaria Commission (1983–87) was set up to examine:', opts: ['Centre-state financial relations and distribution of taxes', 'Centre-state relations in terms of legislative, administrative, and financial powers', 'Reorganisation of states on linguistic basis', 'Panchayati Raj institutions and their powers'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Sarkaria Commission was appointed in 1983 to examine and review the working of existing arrangements between the union and states in legislative, administrative, and financial spheres, and recommend changes within the framework of the Constitution.' },
  { q: 'In India, the power to impose and collect income tax is vested in:', opts: ['State governments', 'Local bodies', 'The Union government, with proceeds shared with states', 'Both Union and states separately on their own citizens'], ans: 2, exp: 'Income tax on incomes other than agricultural income is a Union List subject (Entry 82). The Union levies and collects it, but a constitutionally mandated share is devolved to states through the Finance Commission formula, reflecting cooperative fiscal federalism.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is an example of a concurrent subject in India?', opts: ['Defence', 'Police', 'Education (after 1976)', 'Foreign affairs'], ans: 2, exp: 'Education was originally in the State List but was moved to the Concurrent List by the 42nd Amendment (1976), meaning both Parliament and state legislatures can legislate on it. Defence and foreign affairs are Union subjects; police is a State subject.' },
  { q: 'Under which Article can the President impose President\'s Rule in a state, undermining federalism temporarily?', opts: ['Article 123', 'Article 249', 'Article 356', 'Article 370'], ans: 2, exp: 'Article 356 (President\'s Rule or State Emergency) allows the President to take over the administration of a state if constitutional machinery fails, on the recommendation of the Governor. This temporarily suspends the state government and is considered an anti-federal provision.' },
  { q: 'The S.R. Bommai case (1994) is significant for Indian federalism because it:', opts: ['Granted states the right to secede', 'Curbed arbitrary use of Article 356 (President\'s Rule) by requiring parliamentary approval', 'Created a new category of federal territories', 'Abolished the Concurrent List'], ans: 1, exp: 'In S.R. Bommai vs Union of India (1994), a nine-judge Constitution Bench of the Supreme Court held that the imposition of President\'s Rule is subject to judicial review and requires Lok Sabha approval, thereby curbing its misuse against opposition-ruled states.' },
  { q: 'The Finance Commission of India is constituted under which Article?', opts: ['Article 270', 'Article 280', 'Article 312', 'Article 360'], ans: 1, exp: 'Article 280 of the Constitution requires the President to constitute a Finance Commission every five years. It recommends the distribution of tax revenues between the Union and states and among states, making it a key institution of fiscal federalism.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is CORRECTLY matched regarding the formation of new states in 2000?', opts: ['Jharkhand — from Uttar Pradesh', 'Chhattisgarh — from Bihar', 'Uttarakhand — from Uttar Pradesh', 'Telangana — from Maharashtra'], ans: 2, exp: 'Uttarakhand (then named Uttaranchal) was carved out of Uttar Pradesh in November 2000. Jharkhand was carved from Bihar, and Chhattisgarh from Madhya Pradesh. Telangana was carved from Andhra Pradesh in 2014, not 2000.' },
  { q: 'Belgium\'s capital Brussels has a special status in their federal system because:', opts: ['It is under direct UN administration', 'It is a bilingual region where both French and Dutch speakers have equal rights', 'It belongs exclusively to the Flemish community', 'It is a separate federal unit with its own constitution'], ans: 1, exp: 'Brussels is a bilingual region where both French (Walloon) and Dutch (Flemish) communities are present. Special power-sharing arrangements ensure both communities have equal representation in the Brussels government, reflecting Belgium\'s complex federal architecture.' },
  { q: 'What is the significance of a "rigid constitution" as a feature of federalism?', opts: ['It means the constitution cannot be amended at all', 'It means the division of powers between centre and states cannot be changed by the central government alone — a special procedure involving states is required', 'It means the constitution is printed on hard material', 'It means constitutional cases are decided faster'], ans: 1, exp: 'A rigid constitution ensures that the federal division of powers cannot be altered unilaterally by the central government. Any change affecting the federal structure requires a special majority in Parliament AND ratification by at least half the state legislatures (Article 368), protecting state autonomy.' },
  { q: 'India\'s Constitution places the power to resolve disputes between the centre and states with:', opts: ['The Prime Minister\'s Office', 'The Inter-State Council', 'The Supreme Court of India', 'The Rajya Sabha'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Supreme Court of India has original jurisdiction (Article 131) to adjudicate disputes between the Union and one or more states or between states inter se. This independent judicial arbiter is essential for maintaining the federal balance.' },
  { q: 'The Panchayat Samiti (Intermediate Panchayat) operates at which level?', opts: ['Village level', 'Block/Taluk level', 'District level', 'Sub-division level'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Panchayat Samiti (also called Block Panchayat, Taluk Panchayat, or Mandal Parishad in different states) operates at the Block/Taluka level — the intermediate tier between the gram panchayat (village) and zila parishad (district).' },
  { q: 'Urban local bodies created under the 74th Amendment include all of the following EXCEPT:', opts: ['Municipal Corporation (for large cities)', 'Municipal Council (for smaller urban areas)', 'Nagar Panchayat (for transitional rural-urban areas)', 'Gram Sabha (for urban wards)'], ans: 3, exp: 'The Gram Sabha is a rural institution under the 73rd Amendment. The 74th Amendment established three types of urban local bodies: Nagar Panchayat (for transitional areas), Municipal Council (for smaller towns), and Municipal Corporation (for large cities).' },
  { q: 'The term "decentralisation" as used in Indian federalism means power is distributed across:', opts: ['Only two levels — Union and State', 'Three levels — Union, State, and Local Self-Government', 'Four levels — Union, State, District, Village', 'Only to states from the centre'], ans: 1, exp: 'Decentralisation in the Indian context means power is exercised at three levels: the Union government, state governments, and local self-governments (panchayats and municipalities). The 73rd and 74th Amendments gave constitutional recognition to the third tier.' },
  { q: 'Under Article 243-G, panchayats are empowered to prepare plans for:', opts: ['National defence', 'Economic development and social justice for subjects in the 11th Schedule', 'Union Budget', 'Foreign policy'], ans: 1, exp: 'Article 243-G empowers state legislatures to endow panchayats with powers to prepare and implement plans for economic development and social justice with respect to the 29 matters listed in the 11th Schedule, such as agriculture, education, health, and poverty alleviation.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is a key criticism of India\'s decentralisation despite constitutional provisions?', opts: ['Panchayats have too much money and misuse it', 'States have not effectively devolved funds, functions, and functionaries (3Fs) to local bodies', 'Panchayats have directly challenged state sovereignty', 'Urban municipalities have more power than state governments'], ans: 1, exp: 'Despite the 73rd and 74th Amendments, effective decentralisation remains weak. Many states have not meaningfully transferred the "three Fs" — Funds, Functions, and Functionaries — to local bodies, limiting their capacity to serve as genuine self-governing institutions.' },
  { q: 'The principle that "both levels of government should agree to any change in the power-sharing arrangements" reflects which federal feature?', opts: ['Administrative federalism', 'Cooperative federalism', 'Supremacy of the constitution', 'Decentralisation'], ans: 2, exp: 'The need for both levels of government to agree to changes in power-sharing reflects the supremacy and rigidity of the constitution. In India, constitutional amendments affecting the federal structure require both a special parliamentary majority and ratification by half the states.' },
  { q: 'Zila Parishad operates at which level of governance?', opts: ['Village level', 'Block level', 'District level', 'State level'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Zila Parishad (District Panchayat) is the apex body of the three-tier Panchayati Raj system, operating at the district level. It coordinates the work of Panchayat Samitis, undertakes development work, and administers funds received from the state government.' },
  { q: 'The linguistic reorganisation of states was OPPOSED by which leader who feared it would encourage separatism?', opts: ['B.R. Ambedkar', 'Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel', 'Jawaharlal Nehru', 'Subhash Chandra Bose'], ans: 2, exp: 'Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru and Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel were initially opposed to linguistic reorganisation, fearing it would lead to disintegration of the country along linguistic lines. However, after the death of Potti Sriramulu following a hunger strike for a Telugu state, Andhra Pradesh was created in 1953.' },
  { q: 'Which movement and event directly led to the formation of Andhra Pradesh as the first linguistic state in 1953?', opts: ['Demand by the Kannada-speaking community', 'Potti Sriramulu\'s fast unto death demanding a separate Telugu state', 'Armed uprising in the Telangana region', 'Recommendation of the Fazl Ali Commission'], ans: 1, exp: 'Potti Sriramulu undertook a fast unto death demanding a separate state for Telugu speakers. His death in December 1952 triggered widespread unrest, forcing the government to create Andhra Pradesh in October 1953, paving the way for the broader linguistic reorganisation of 1956.' },
  { q: 'The Inter-State Council (Article 263) was set up to:', opts: ['Adjudicate inter-state river water disputes', 'Promote coordination and resolve disputes between states and between states and the Union', 'Approve constitutional amendments affecting states', 'Control inter-state trade and commerce'], ans: 1, exp: 'Article 263 allows the President to establish an Inter-State Council to inquire into and advise on inter-state disputes, to investigate subjects of common interest, and to make recommendations for better coordination of policy and action. It was established in 1990.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following features of India\'s constitution ensures that the federal structure cannot be destroyed by the centre?', opts: ['Parliamentary supremacy', 'Judicial review by an independent Supreme Court', 'Discretionary powers of the Governor', 'Article 356 President\'s Rule'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Supreme Court\'s power of judicial review (the ability to strike down laws and executive actions that violate the Constitution) is the ultimate safeguard of Indian federalism. It ensures neither the centre nor states can transgress constitutional limits unilaterally.' },
];

// CH19_SOCIAL (Civics Ch3 — Gender, Religion and Caste)
const SOCIAL_CH19 = [
  { q: 'The "sexual division of labour" refers to:', opts: ['A social arrangement where most household and childcare work is assigned to women, while men do outside work', 'Equal distribution of household tasks between men and women', 'Biological differences determining professional abilities', 'Legal separation of male and female workplaces'], ans: 0, exp: 'The sexual division of labour is not a natural but a social arrangement where, by convention and patriarchal norms, domestic and caring work is assigned predominantly to women (unpaid) while paid productive work outside the home is considered men\'s domain.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "patriarchy" in the context of gender studies means:', opts: ['A society governed by the oldest male in the family', 'Societies where only men can vote', 'Biological male characteristics', 'A system of male domination in which men hold power in family, society, and public life'], ans: 3, exp: 'Patriarchy refers to a social system in which men hold primary power in political leadership, moral authority, social privilege, and control of property. It shapes how families, institutions, and societies operate — systematically privileging men over women.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following BEST describes the "Triple Burden" faced by women?', opts: ['Tax burden, electoral burden, and educational burden', 'Caste discrimination, gender discrimination, and class discrimination', 'Reproductive work (home/care), productive work (paid employment), and community/social work', 'Poverty, illiteracy, and lack of healthcare'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Triple Burden theory (from feminist economics) argues women bear three simultaneous burdens: reproductive work (domestic/care unpaid), productive work (wage employment, often part-time or informal), and community managing (social care work). This leaves them with less time, lower wages, and fewer political opportunities.' },
  { q: 'The gender wage gap in India means that women on average:', opts: ['Earn more than men in the same profession due to affirmative action', 'Have higher income because of welfare schemes', 'Earn exactly the same as men due to equal pay laws', 'Earn significantly less than men for equal work across most sectors'], ans: 3, exp: 'Despite the Equal Remuneration Act (1976), the gender wage gap persists in India. Women earn approximately 20–30% less than men for similar work, partly due to occupational segregation, interrupted careers, unpaid domestic labour, and discrimination.' },
  { q: 'In the Indian Parliament (Lok Sabha), what is the approximate percentage of women MPs as of the 17th Lok Sabha (elected 2019)?', opts: ['5%', '14%', '33%', '50%'], ans: 1, exp: 'Women\'s representation in the 17th Lok Sabha (2019) reached about 14.4% — the highest ever but still far below the globally recommended 33%. The Women\'s Reservation Bill (108th Amendment) demanding 33% reservation in Parliament remains pending.' },
  { q: 'The Women\'s Reservation Bill seeks to reserve what percentage of seats for women in Parliament and state assemblies?', opts: ['15%', '25%', '33%', '50%'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Women\'s Reservation Bill (Constitution 108th Amendment Bill) proposes 33% reservation for women in the Lok Sabha and state legislative assemblies. It has been passed by the Rajya Sabha (2010) but long remained pending in Lok Sabha, with the Constitution (106th Amendment) Act, 2023 finally passing it though deferring implementation.' },
  { q: 'Self Help Groups (SHGs) for women primarily aim to:', opts: ['Enable women to collectively save, access microcredit, and achieve economic independence — empowering them', 'Train women for government jobs only', 'Replace panchayat institutions in villages', 'Lobby for women\'s reservation in Parliament'], ans: 0, exp: 'Women\'s SHGs pool small savings, access bank credit collectively, and invest in income-generating activities. They build financial independence, social solidarity, confidence, and political awareness among poor rural women — a major tool for women\'s empowerment.' },
  { q: 'The feminist movement in India has historically demanded all of the following EXCEPT:', opts: ['Equal wages for equal work', 'Right to vote and stand for election', 'Separate personal laws for different religions', 'Protection from domestic violence'], ans: 2, exp: 'The feminist movement has demanded equal wages, voting rights, political representation, protection from violence, and education. Separate personal laws based on religion are a communal demand, not a feminist one — in fact, many feminists argue for a Uniform Civil Code for gender equality.' },
  { q: 'In India, women\'s reservation in panchayats at not less than 1/3 of seats was mandated by the:', opts: ['42nd Amendment', '52nd Amendment', '73rd Amendment', '86th Amendment'], ans: 2, exp: 'The 73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992) under Article 243-D mandated reservation of not less than one-third of total seats and offices of chairpersons in panchayats for women, resulting in approximately 1.4 million elected women representatives in local bodies.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is the most accurate definition of "communalism" in the Indian political context?', opts: ['Promotion of community development programmes', 'Celebration of religious festivals publicly', 'Constructing places of worship with government funds', 'Using religious identity to mobilise voters and pursue political power at the expense of other religious communities'], ans: 3, exp: 'Communalism is the ideology that religious identity forms the primary political identity, that members of one religion have common political interests opposed to those of another religion. When religion is used to mobilise people for political or electoral purposes — often with antagonism toward other communities — it is communalism.' },
  { q: 'Which Article of the Indian Constitution abolished untouchability?', opts: ['Article 14', 'Article 15', 'Article 17', 'Article 21'], ans: 2, exp: 'Article 17 of the Constitution of India abolishes "Untouchability" and forbids its practice in any form. Enforcement of any disability arising out of "Untouchability" shall be an offence punishable in accordance with law (Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955).' },
  { q: 'The concept of a "secular state" as embedded in the Indian Constitution means:', opts: ['The state has its own official religion for governance', 'The state bans religious practice in public spaces', 'The state does not have an official religion, treats all religions equally, and does not discriminate on grounds of religion', 'The state controls the internal management of all religious institutions'], ans: 2, exp: 'India\'s secular state — as in the Preamble (since 42nd Amendment, 1976) and Articles 25–28 — means no state religion, equal respect and protection for all religions, freedom of religious practice for individuals, and no state discrimination on grounds of religion.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following BEST distinguishes personal religious belief from communal politics?', opts: ['Personal belief is private and does not seek political power; communal politics uses religious identity to mobilise power, often in opposition to other communities', 'They are identical — all religious belief is inherently political', 'Communal politics refers only to terrorist activities', 'Personal belief and communal politics both require religious conversion'], ans: 0, exp: 'Personal religious belief is a private matter of faith and practice, posing no threat to others. Communal politics, in contrast, uses religious identity to create "us vs. them" divisions, mobilise votes, and pursue group interests at the expense of political harmony and minority rights.' },
  { q: 'B.R. Ambedkar\'s contribution to addressing caste discrimination includes:', opts: ['Advocating for the continuance of the caste system for social order', 'Founding the Indian National Congress', 'Opposing reservations for Dalits in education', 'Leading the movement for the rights of Dalits, writing the Constitution with provisions against untouchability, and converting to Buddhism'], ans: 3, exp: 'B.R. Ambedkar (Babasaheb) was a Dalit leader and the principal drafter of the Indian Constitution. He fought for Dalit rights, led the Mahad March (1927), advocated reservations, and ultimately converted to Buddhism with thousands of followers in 1956 as a rejection of the caste system.' },
  { q: 'E.V. Ramasamy "Periyar" is associated with which social reform movement?', opts: ['Brahmo Samaj in Bengal', 'Arya Samaj in Punjab', 'Self-Respect Movement and the Dravidian movement in Tamil Nadu', 'Satya Shodhak Samaj in Maharashtra'], ans: 2, exp: 'Periyar (E.V. Ramasamy Naicker) founded the Self-Respect Movement in 1925 in Tamil Nadu, which fought against Brahminical dominance, caste discrimination, and gender inequality, demanding rational self-respect for non-Brahmin communities. He also founded the Dravidar Kazhagam.' },
  { q: 'The "vote bank" politics in the context of caste refers to:', opts: ['Scheduled Caste communities not being allowed to vote', 'Banks that fund electoral campaigns', 'Electronic voting machines in rural areas', 'Political parties mobilising voters of a particular caste as a consolidated bloc to win elections'], ans: 3, exp: 'Vote bank politics refers to the practice of political parties courting specific caste, religious, or ethnic groups as a reliable bloc of voters through promises of patronage, reservations, or symbolic representation, rather than on the basis of policies and governance.' },
  { q: 'The OBC (Other Backward Classes) category in India was given political salience after:', opts: ['Implementation of the Mandal Commission recommendations in 1990 under V.P. Singh\'s government', 'The Nehru era policies of the 1950s', 'The 73rd Amendment of 1992', 'The Emergency period of 1975–77'], ans: 0, exp: 'The Mandal Commission (1979–80) recommended 27% reservation for OBCs in central government jobs and educational institutions. V.P. Singh\'s government announced its implementation in 1990, triggering nationwide debate and protests, and politically consolidating OBC identity.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is the BEST description of "casteism"?', opts: ['Recognising the existence of castes for academic study', 'Providing caste-based reservations in jobs', 'The belief that one\'s own caste is superior and acting to promote caste interests in social and political life at the expense of others', 'Maintaining caste records for census purposes'], ans: 2, exp: 'Casteism is the prejudice, discrimination, or antagonism directed against people of a different caste based on the belief that one\'s own caste is superior. It manifests in political mobilisation along caste lines, social discrimination, and perpetuation of caste hierarchies.' },
  { q: 'The NCERT text argues that caste and politics interact in three ways. Which of the following is NOT one of them?', opts: ['Caste influences who gets to vote and contest elections', 'Political parties choose candidates considering the caste composition of constituencies', 'Every caste group tries to get the maximum representation in government', 'Governments legislate to enforce caste separation in public spaces'], ans: 3, exp: 'NCERT identifies that caste influences politics through: caste as a unit of political mobilisation, parties selecting candidates on caste calculations, and caste groups seeking government representation and benefits. However, governments enforcing caste separation is not a feature of democracy — in fact the Constitution prohibits it.' },
  { q: 'The term "Scheduled Castes" in the Indian Constitution refers to:', opts: ['All backward communities in India', 'Only those communities that have converted from Hinduism', 'Communities listed in the Concurrent List', 'Communities listed in a presidential schedule that have suffered severe social disabilities and untouchability, entitled to reservations'], ans: 3, exp: 'Scheduled Castes (SCs) are communities notified under Article 341 of the Constitution — primarily those that have historically suffered caste-based discrimination and untouchability. They are entitled to reservations in education, employment, and legislative bodies under Articles 15(4), 16(4), and 330/332.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is an example of gender inequality in the workforce in India?', opts: ['Women are disproportionately employed in low-paying, insecure, informal sector jobs while men dominate formal sector', 'Women and men have identical labour force participation rates', 'Men and women receive the same average salary across all sectors', 'Women\'s share in the formal workforce exceeds men in urban India'], ans: 0, exp: 'In India, women\'s labour force participation rate is much lower than men\'s. Women who work are disproportionately in the informal sector, domestic labour, and agricultural work — with lower wages, less job security, and fewer legal protections than men in the formal economy.' },
  { q: 'Religious pluralism as enshrined in India\'s Constitution means:', opts: ['One religion is preferred but others are tolerated', 'Religious conversion is prohibited', 'Each religion has equal rights and the state does not favour any religion', 'All religious practices are regulated by a central religious authority'], ans: 2, exp: 'India\'s Constitution guarantees every individual the freedom of conscience and the right to profess, practise, and propagate religion (Article 25). No religion has state sponsorship. Articles 25–28 collectively ensure freedom of religion and religious pluralism.' },
  { q: 'The slogan "Jai Bhim" is associated with which movement?', opts: ['Dalit movement inspired by B.R. Ambedkar', 'Farmers\' movement in Punjab', 'Women\'s movement in Kerala', 'OBC movement in Bihar'], ans: 0, exp: '"Jai Bhim" (Victory to Bhim — a reference to B.R. Ambedkar) is a salutation and rallying cry used by Dalits, Ambedkarites, and supporters of the movement for Dalit rights and social justice, expressing solidarity with Ambedkar\'s vision of an egalitarian, casteless society.' },
  { q: 'India is described as a secular state because it does all of the following EXCEPT:', opts: ['Ensures no state religion', 'Treats all religions with equal respect', 'Does not discriminate against citizens on grounds of religion', 'Prohibits individuals from practising their religion privately'], ans: 3, exp: 'India\'s secularism does not mean banning individual religious practice. Article 25 guarantees the right to freedom of religion. Secularism means the state has no official religion, treats all equally, and does not discriminate — it does not prohibit private religious practice.' },
  { q: 'Communal riots are most likely to occur in a context where:', opts: ['There is equal representation of all communities in government', 'Government provides equal economic development to all communities', 'There is freedom of press and independent media', 'Political leaders mobilise communities against each other for electoral gains, building atmosphere of fear and mistrust'], ans: 3, exp: 'Communal violence typically erupts where political entrepreneurs deliberately build inter-community mistrust and fear for electoral mobilisation, often using propaganda, incidents of provocation, and systematic dehumanisation of the "other" community, showing communalism\'s destructive political dimension.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "glass ceiling" in the context of women\'s employment refers to:', opts: ['Invisible but real barriers that prevent women from reaching top positions despite qualification', 'Transparent office partitions mandated in workplaces', 'Laws restricting women\'s working hours', 'Quotas limiting women\'s entry into certain professions'], ans: 0, exp: 'The "glass ceiling" is a metaphor for the invisible barriers — caused by gender discrimination, stereotypes, and lack of networks — that prevent qualified women from advancing to senior managerial and leadership positions, even when they have the required skills and experience.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following practices represents religion being used in a POSITIVE, non-communal way in politics?', opts: ['Demanding that a political party be composed entirely of members of one religion', 'Using communal riots to intimidate minority voters before elections', 'Claiming government funds only for one community\'s religious institutions', 'A political leader invoking religious ethics like non-violence and truth to argue for peace policies'], ans: 3, exp: 'NCERT distinguishes between religion influencing politics positively (invoking universal religious ethics — compassion, truth, non-violence — to guide policies) versus communalism, which uses religious identity to divide and dominate. Gandhi\'s use of religious moral principles in politics is an example of the former.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "women\'s empowerment" as discussed in social science means:', opts: ['Giving women luxury goods and entertainment', 'Recruiting women into the armed forces only', 'Men giving up their jobs in favour of women', 'Enabling women to have control over their own lives through economic independence, education, legal rights, and political voice'], ans: 3, exp: 'Women\'s empowerment is a multi-dimensional process by which women gain greater control over their own lives — through access to education, economic opportunities, property rights, freedom from violence, political participation, and decision-making power in family and community.' },
  { q: 'Jyotirao Phule\'s Satya Shodhak Samaj (1873) was primarily aimed at:', opts: ['Promoting Aryan culture and Sanskrit education', 'Liberating low-caste communities from Brahminical dominance and promoting education for all', 'Demanding a separate Maratha state', 'Reforming upper-caste marriage practices only'], ans: 1, exp: 'Jyotirao Phule founded the Satya Shodhak Samaj (Truth-Seekers\' Society) in 1873 in Maharashtra to liberate Shudra and Dalit communities from Brahminical dominance, promote education for lower castes and women, and challenge caste hierarchy. He is considered a pioneer of social reform in Maharashtra.' },
  { q: 'In India\'s political system, caste has moved from a traditional hierarchy to a democratic context in which:', opts: ['Lower castes have used democratic politics to challenge upper-caste dominance and demand rights and representation', 'Caste has entirely disappeared from political discourse', 'Caste only operates in rural areas and not in urban politics', 'The judiciary has abolished caste consideration in politics'], ans: 0, exp: 'Democracy has transformed caste from a purely hierarchical social institution. Lower castes — OBCs, SCs, and STs — have organised politically, formed parties, and used electoral power to demand representation, reservations, and social justice, challenging traditional Brahmin/upper-caste dominance.' },
  { q: 'The Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955 was enacted to enforce the constitutional abolition of untouchability under:', opts: ['Article 14', 'Article 15', 'Article 17', 'Article 23'], ans: 2, exp: 'Article 17 of the Constitution abolished untouchability. To give teeth to this provision, Parliament enacted the Untouchability (Offences) Act, 1955 — later renamed the Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955 — which penalises enforcement of any disability arising from untouchability.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is a correct statement about reservation policy in India?', opts: ['Reservations guarantee equality of outcomes for all groups', 'Reservations in Parliament have already reached 33% for women', 'Reservations are provided only in the private sector', 'Reservations are a temporary affirmative action measure in the Constitution for SCs, STs, and OBCs to ensure representation and opportunity'], ans: 3, exp: 'India\'s reservation policy — Articles 15(4), 16(4), 330/332 — provides affirmative action for SCs (15%), STs (7.5%), and OBCs (27%) in government jobs and educational institutions. It is meant to provide equal opportunity, not equality of outcome. Women\'s parliamentary reservation is aspirational, not yet achieved.' },
  { q: 'The demand for a Uniform Civil Code (UCC) is linked to gender equality because:', opts: ['A UCC would impose a single taxation system', 'A UCC would abolish all religious practices', 'A UCC would replace religion-based personal laws — which often disadvantage women in marriage, divorce, and inheritance — with a common gender-just code', 'A UCC would enforce the death penalty uniformly'], ans: 2, exp: 'Personal laws in India — governed separately by religious community for marriage, divorce, inheritance, adoption — often systematically disadvantage women. A Uniform Civil Code advocates replacing these with a common, gender-equal civil law for all citizens regardless of religion.' },
  { q: 'The term "minority rights" in a democratic context means:', opts: ['Minorities have more rights than the majority', 'Minorities can opt out of constitutional obligations', 'Minority communities can establish a separate government', 'Minorities should have equal rights and additional protections to ensure they are not oppressed by the majority'], ans: 3, exp: 'Minority rights mean ensuring that individuals belonging to minority groups (religious, linguistic, ethnic) enjoy the same rights as others AND have special protections (Articles 29–30 in India) to maintain their culture, language, and educational institutions, preventing majority domination.' },
  { q: 'Which Article of the Indian Constitution guarantees religious minorities the right to establish educational institutions?', opts: ['Article 25', 'Article 28', 'Article 30', 'Article 46'], ans: 2, exp: 'Article 30 guarantees all minorities (religious or linguistic) the right to establish and administer educational institutions of their choice. This is a special protection for minority communities to preserve their culture and language, and such institutions are also entitled to government grants on a non-discriminatory basis.' },
  { q: 'Social reform movements of the 19th and 20th centuries in India addressed gender issues by:', opts: ['Supporting child marriage and sati as cultural traditions', 'Opposing sati, advocating widow remarriage, promoting women\'s education and opposing purdah and dowry', 'Demanding women remain exclusively in the domestic sphere', 'Opposing women\'s suffrage movement'], ans: 1, exp: '19th-century reform movements (Raja Ram Mohan Roy, Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar, etc.) campaigned against sati and child marriage, for widow remarriage and women\'s education. 20th-century movements continued with anti-dowry campaigns, women\'s suffrage, and anti-purdah advocacy.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "secularism" in India differs from Western secularism in that:', opts: ['Indian secularism is identical to European secularism of strict separation of church and state', 'Indian secularism means the state keeps a distance from all religion equally — but also intervenes to ensure equal rights across communities and reform oppressive religious practices', 'Indian secularism means the state promotes all religions equally with funding', 'Indian secularism bans religious expression in all public spaces'], ans: 1, exp: 'Indian secularism is "principled equidistance" — the state neither merges with nor completely separates from religion. Unlike French laïcité (strict separation), Indian secularism allows the state to intervene in religious matters to ensure equality and social reform (e.g., abolition of untouchability).' },
  { q: 'Honour killing is an example of:', opts: ['Religious reformation', 'Patriarchal violence used to enforce caste and gender norms — particularly against those who marry outside caste or seek individual choice', 'Legal tribal custom protected by the Constitution', 'Government-sanctioned practice'], ans: 1, exp: 'Honour killing — the murder of a person (usually a woman) by family members for perceived violation of family honour, often for inter-caste or inter-religious marriage — is an extreme manifestation of patriarchal and casteist violence. It is a criminal offence and unconstitutional.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following correctly describes the linkage between poverty, caste, and gender in India?', opts: ['They are completely separate issues with no interaction', 'Women from lower castes face "intersectional" disadvantage — experiencing both caste discrimination and gender discrimination simultaneously', 'Only caste matters in determining poverty outcomes in India', 'Gender discrimination exists only among upper castes'], ans: 1, exp: 'Intersectionality captures how multiple axes of disadvantage — caste, gender, class — interact. A Dalit woman faces both caste-based discrimination (from society and upper castes) and gender-based discrimination (from men within and outside her community), creating compounded, overlapping forms of oppression.' },
  { q: 'The Mandal Commission officially known as the Backward Classes Commission was set up in:', opts: ['1969', '1971', '1978', '1990'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Second Backward Classes Commission (popularly called the Mandal Commission after its chairman B.P. Mandal) was established by the Janata Party government in 1978. It submitted its report in 1980, recommending 27% reservation for OBCs in central government services and public sector undertakings.' },
  { q: 'The feminist critique of mainstream economics for ignoring women\'s work centres on the fact that:', opts: ['Women earn more than men but it is not counted', 'Unpaid domestic and care work performed by women is not counted in GDP, making women\'s economic contribution invisible', 'Women\'s productive agricultural work is counted twice', 'Women\'s market income is taxed more heavily'], ans: 1, exp: 'Feminist economists argue that GDP-based national accounting ignores unpaid household work (cooking, childcare, elder care) performed predominantly by women. This invisibility perpetuates the undervaluation of women\'s labour and makes the true scale of women\'s economic contribution to society impossible to measure.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes "communal harmony" as distinct from communalism?', opts: ['Communal harmony means each community living in a separate territory', 'Communal harmony involves peaceful, respectful coexistence of different religious communities with shared public spaces and civic life', 'Communal harmony means religious communities cannot interact', 'Communal harmony requires one community to adopt the practices of another'], ans: 1, exp: 'Communal harmony means members of different religious communities living side by side with mutual respect, shared civic institutions, and peaceful coexistence — as opposed to communalism, which creates divisions, fosters fear, and uses religion for hostile political mobilisation.' },
  { q: 'The case of "caste-based electoral mobilisation" can be seen as:', opts: ['Purely negative — caste must never influence electoral behaviour', 'A complex phenomenon — while it can perpetuate divisions, it has also enabled marginalised groups to organise and gain political representation', 'Evidence that India is not a true democracy', 'Proof that reservations should be abolished'], ans: 1, exp: 'Caste in elections is paradoxical: it can reinforce divisions and result in vote bank manipulation, but it has also empowered historically marginalised castes (OBCs, SCs) to mobilise, contest elections, and demand rights. The challenge for democracy is to transcend caste while ensuring fair representation.' },
  { q: 'Anganwadi workers and ASHA workers in India are predominantly women working in the informal sector. This illustrates:', opts: ['Equal opportunity for women in government employment', 'How women are concentrated in low-paid "care economy" work even in government schemes, reflecting the feminisation of poorly compensated labour', 'That women prefer part-time government work', 'That gender equality has been achieved in public health'], ans: 1, exp: 'Anganwadi workers (ICDS scheme) and ASHA workers (National Health Mission) are almost exclusively women, paid as "honorary workers" (not employees) with minimal, irregular remuneration despite crucial public health responsibilities. This reflects the consistent devaluation of care work when performed by women.' },
  { q: 'The Indian Constitution prohibits caste-based discrimination but caste persists. The NCERT text attributes this to:', opts: ['A weakness in the constitutional provisions', 'The gap between constitutional ideals and social reality — deep-rooted historical inequality not easily erased by legal provisions alone', 'Lack of awareness of constitutional rights among upper castes', 'International pressure to maintain caste'], ans: 1, exp: 'While the Constitution abolishes caste-based discrimination, India\'s deeply entrenched caste system — evolved over millennia — persists in practice. Social transformation requires not just legal provisions but social reform, education, economic empowerment, and political will to change entrenched attitudes.' },
  { q: 'Which constitutional Article gives both men and women the right to vote at age 18 without gender discrimination?', opts: ['Article 16', 'Article 21', 'Article 326', 'Article 330'], ans: 2, exp: 'Article 326 provides that elections to Lok Sabha and state legislative assemblies shall be on the basis of adult suffrage — every citizen above 18 has the right to vote without discrimination on the basis of religion, race, caste, sex, etc. This was gender-neutral from the Constitution\'s adoption in 1950.' },
  { q: 'The term "gender" in social science refers to:', opts: ['Biological sex (male/female)', 'The social, cultural, and psychological differences between men and women as constructed by society', 'Genetic characteristics only', 'Legal classification for census purposes'], ans: 1, exp: 'In social science, "sex" refers to biological characteristics (male/female), while "gender" refers to the social and cultural roles, expectations, behaviours, and identities associated with being a man or woman. Gender is a social construction that varies across cultures and historical periods.' },
  { q: 'The Domestic Violence Act (Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005) was a legislative milestone because it:', opts: ['Punished women for leaving the home', 'Extended legal protection to women against physical, emotional, economic, and sexual abuse within the home — recognising private sphere violence as a legal matter', 'Only protected married women from violence by strangers', 'Criminalised all disagreements within households'], ans: 1, exp: 'The DV Act (2005) is landmark legislation recognising that violence against women in the domestic sphere — including emotional, economic, and sexual abuse (not just physical) — is a public legal matter, not a private family affair. It provides civil remedies including protection orders and residence orders.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following CORRECTLY describes how caste interacts with economic class in India?', opts: ['Caste and class are completely unrelated social categories', 'Historically, lower castes have been disproportionately poor because caste hierarchy denied them property, education, and skilled occupations — caste and class overlap significantly', 'Upper castes are always economically rich', 'Class distinctions have completely replaced caste distinctions in modern India'], ans: 1, exp: 'Caste and class are distinct but overlapping. Centuries of caste-based exclusion from land ownership, skilled trades, and education have meant lower castes are disproportionately poor. Economic liberalisation has created a new wealthy OBC/SC class, but the overlap between low caste and poverty remains significant.' },
  { q: 'The National Commission for Women (NCW) was established to:', opts: ['Manage women\'s reservations in Parliament', 'Review constitutional and legal safeguards for women, investigate violations, and recommend remedial action to the government', 'Supervise SHG microcredit programmes', 'Represent India in international women\'s forums exclusively'], ans: 1, exp: 'The National Commission for Women was established by the NCW Act, 1990. It reviews constitutional and legal provisions for women, investigates cases of violations of women\'s rights, organises awareness programmes, and advises the government on policy — a statutory body for women\'s rights protection.' },
];

// CH20_SOCIAL (Civics Ch5 — Outcomes of Democracy)
const SOCIAL_CH20 = [
  { q: 'The most fundamental argument for preferring democracy over dictatorship is that democracy:', opts: ['Always produces faster economic growth', 'Provides a principled mechanism for peaceful transfer of power and accountable governance', 'Guarantees equal incomes for all citizens', 'Eliminates all forms of corruption'], ans: 1, exp: 'The most fundamental argument for democracy is not economic — dictatorships sometimes grow faster in the short term. Rather, democracy\'s core strength is that it provides institutionalised mechanisms for peaceful power transfer, accountability of rulers, and protection of rights, giving it legitimacy that dictatorships lack.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is the MOST accurate assessment of democracy\'s economic record compared to dictatorships?', opts: ['Democracies always grow faster than dictatorships', 'Dictatorships always grow faster than democracies', 'There is no clear pattern — some dictatorships have grown fast, but democracies produce more equitable economic outcomes', 'Democracies never develop economically'], ans: 2, exp: 'Research shows no consistent link between regime type and growth rate. Some authoritarian states (East Asian "tiger" economies) grew rapidly under dictatorship. However, democracies tend to produce more equitable distribution of growth and better social development outcomes, and avoid catastrophic famines (Amartya Sen\'s famous argument).' },
  { q: 'Amartya Sen\'s argument that "no substantial famine has ever occurred in a functioning multiparty democracy" highlights which outcome of democracy?', opts: ['Democracy always produces the highest agricultural yields', 'A free press and accountable government in democracy create political incentives to prevent famines that dictatorships lack', 'Democracy ensures equal land distribution', 'Democratic governments always have larger food reserves'], ans: 1, exp: 'Nobel laureate Amartya Sen argued that famines are not caused by food shortage alone but by policy failure. Democratic governments — subject to a free press, opposition parties, and elections — face political costs for allowing famines, creating incentives to respond to food crises. Dictatorships can suppress such information.' },
  { q: 'The Right to Information (RTI) Act, 2005 contributes to the outcomes of democracy by:', opts: ['Allowing citizens to change government policy directly', 'Making government information accessible to citizens, enabling transparency and accountability', 'Giving media houses exclusive access to classified documents', 'Allowing private companies to audit government accounts'], ans: 1, exp: 'The RTI Act (2005) operationalises democratic accountability by empowering citizens to demand information from public authorities within 30 days. It exposes corruption, arbitrary decision-making, and inefficiency, making government transparent and accountable — a key democratic outcome.' },
  { q: 'Democratic legitimacy means that a democratic government is legitimate because:', opts: ['It has the largest army and can enforce its will', 'It derives authority from the free consent of the governed, expressed through regular elections', 'The richest citizens have approved its formation', 'It has been in power for the longest period'], ans: 1, exp: 'Legitimacy in a democracy is the recognition by citizens that a government has the right to rule because it was chosen through free and fair elections. This consent-based legitimacy means people are more likely to accept government decisions — even unpopular ones — as binding.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes "responsive government" as an outcome of democracy?', opts: ['A government that responds only to the demands of the majority community', 'A government that is sensitive and reacts to the needs, demands, and feedback of all its citizens', 'A government that responds exclusively to judicial orders', 'A government that responds only to international pressure'], ans: 1, exp: 'A responsive government — a key democratic outcome — anticipates and reacts to citizens\' needs and aspirations across all sections, not just the powerful. Electoral accountability incentivises democratic leaders to attend to public welfare, unlike authoritarian rulers insulated from popular feedback.' },
  { q: 'NOTA (None of the Above) was introduced in Indian elections in:', opts: ['2005', '2009', '2013', '2017'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Supreme Court directed the Election Commission of India in PUCL vs Union of India (2013) to provide voters with the option to vote for "None of the Above" (NOTA) on the EVM. This was first used in the November 2013 state assembly elections, giving voters a formal way to reject all candidates.' },
  { q: 'What is the primary significance of NOTA in the democratic process?', opts: ['It removes the winning candidate and triggers a re-election automatically', 'It allows voters to formally express dissatisfaction with all candidates without abstaining, capturing protest votes within the electoral system', 'It gives an additional seat to the second-place candidate', 'It only applies in by-elections'], ans: 1, exp: 'NOTA is significant as a mechanism for voters to express dissatisfaction with all available candidates. While NOTA votes do not affect the result (the highest-polling candidate still wins), it adds a layer of accountability by making protest visible within the electoral system, pressuring parties to field better candidates.' },
  { q: 'Democracy is said to produce "legitimate decisions" because:', opts: ['Democratic decisions are always morally correct', 'Even if slow or imperfect, democratic decisions carry legitimacy because they follow transparent processes of consultation and consent', 'Democracies never take wrong decisions', 'All democratic decisions must be approved by international bodies'], ans: 1, exp: 'Democratic decision-making — through consultation, debate, deliberation, and majority rule with minority protection — takes longer but produces decisions that citizens are more likely to accept as legitimate, even when they disagree, because the process was fair. This contrasts with arbitrary dictatorial decrees.' },
  { q: 'The "accountable government" aspect of democracy means:', opts: ['Government is only accountable to the judiciary', 'Government officials must explain and justify their decisions to citizens, can be questioned by Parliament, media, and civil society, and removed through elections', 'Accountants manage government finances independently', 'Government accounts are audited by a foreign agency'], ans: 1, exp: 'Accountable government is a core democratic outcome. In democracy, rulers must explain their actions to the public — through parliamentary question hours, RTI requests, free media scrutiny, judicial oversight, and ultimately electoral accountability. This multi-layered accountability is absent in dictatorships.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is the BEST measure of whether a democracy promotes social equality?', opts: ['GDP per capita growth rate', 'Gini coefficient (income inequality measure) and access to public services across different social groups', 'Military strength', 'Number of laws passed by Parliament'], ans: 1, exp: 'The quality of social equality in a democracy is best measured by outcome indicators — income inequality (Gini coefficient), access to education, healthcare, and justice across different social groups (castes, gender, religion). High economic growth with high inequality represents a poor democratic outcome for equity.' },
  { q: 'Democracy is considered better than dictatorship at managing social diversity because:', opts: ['Democracy suppresses minority identities for national unity', 'Democracy provides institutional channels — elections, representation, free speech — for diverse groups to negotiate and resolve conflicts peacefully', 'Democracy requires all citizens to follow the same culture', 'Democracy eliminates ethnic and religious differences over time'], ans: 1, exp: 'Democracy manages diversity through inclusion — by giving diverse groups representation, voice, and legal protection, reducing the likelihood that conflicts escalate into violence. Dictatorships often manage diversity through suppression, which stores up resentments that erupt later.' },
  { q: 'Public perception surveys in India show that many citizens are dissatisfied with how democracy functions. This means:', opts: ['India should replace democracy with a dictatorship', 'Citizens distinguish between support for democracy as a system and dissatisfaction with how their particular democratic government performs — the latter does not negate the former', 'Indian democracy is a complete failure', 'Voter turnout in India is always low due to this dissatisfaction'], ans: 1, exp: 'Survey data (including CSDS surveys) show Indians broadly support democracy as a preferred system of governance even while being critical of corruption, inefficiency, and lack of representation. This "dissatisfied democrat" pattern is a sign of maturing democratic expectations, not rejection of democracy.' },
  { q: 'India\'s voter turnout trends show that:', opts: ['Voter turnout has consistently declined since 1952', 'Poorer and marginalised voters often participate in higher numbers than the elite, showing genuine democratic investment in the system', 'Only educated urban voters participate in large numbers', 'Voter turnout in India is among the lowest in the world'], ans: 1, exp: 'Contrary to typical expectations, electoral studies in India show that poorer, lower-caste, and rural voters often vote in higher percentages than wealthier, urban, upper-caste voters. This reflects that marginalised groups view elections as a meaningful opportunity for change, a sign of substantive democratic participation.' },
  { q: 'The "dignity of women" as an outcome of democracy refers to:', opts: ['Women receiving luxury goods from the government', 'Legal equality, freedom from discrimination and violence, and the right of women to make decisions about their own lives — guaranteed by democratic constitutions and laws', 'Women\'s participation in beauty pageants', 'Special economic zones for women entrepreneurs'], ans: 1, exp: 'Democracy offers women dignity through constitutional equality, legal prohibitions on discrimination and violence, political representation, and property rights. While India still falls short in practice, the democratic framework — unlike many authoritarian systems — provides women legal personhood, voting rights, and access to justice.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is the most significant challenge that democracies face in delivering on their promise of equality?', opts: ['Too many elections causing voter fatigue', 'The gap between formal equality (equal rights in law) and substantive equality (actual equal outcomes in practice)', 'Democracy cannot regulate business and economics', 'Judicial systems interfering with legislation'], ans: 1, exp: 'The central challenge for democracies is converting formal legal equality into substantive equality in real life. Poor, marginalised, or minority citizens may possess legal rights on paper but face systemic barriers — poverty, discrimination, lack of access — that prevent them from enjoying actual equality.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "transparency" as a democratic outcome means:', opts: ['All government buildings must have transparent walls', 'Government processes, decisions, and information are open to public scrutiny, reducing corruption and arbitrary decision-making', 'Transparent ballot boxes in elections', 'Only the Prime Minister\'s decisions need to be publicised'], ans: 1, exp: 'Transparency in democratic governance means that how decisions are made, who makes them, on what basis, and with what public funds is knowable and open to citizens, media, courts, and civil society. This reduces the scope for corruption and makes power more accountable than in closed authoritarian systems.' },
  { q: 'The challenge of "corruption" in Indian democracy suggests that democracy:', opts: ['Has completely failed in India', 'Faces the challenge that elections alone cannot prevent corruption — other institutions like a free press, RTI, independent CBI, and civic activism are also needed', 'Is unnecessary since corruption exists regardless of system', 'Only affects democracies, not authoritarian states'], ans: 1, exp: 'Corruption in Indian democracy illustrates that elections are necessary but not sufficient for good governance. A healthy democracy requires a free press to expose corruption, strong investigative agencies, independent judiciary, active civil society, and RTI — all working together as checks on power.' },
  { q: 'What does the NCERT text suggest when it says democracy should be "evaluated on its own terms"?', opts: ['Democracy should only be compared to other democracies, not authoritarian states', 'Democracy should be judged not only by economic growth but by its achievements in accountability, dignity, rights, and conflict resolution', 'Democracy is always better and needs no evaluation', 'Democracies should set their own rules to avoid external scrutiny'], ans: 1, exp: 'NCERT argues that democracy should be assessed on criteria appropriate to it — not just GDP growth (where dictatorships may sometimes do better short-term) but on accountability, rule of law, protection of rights, dignity, peaceful conflict resolution, and legitimacy, where democracy consistently outperforms authoritarian alternatives.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following would be the BEST evidence that a democracy is producing positive outcomes for minority communities?', opts: ['Minority communities winning elections in seats they do not form a majority', 'Minority communities having equal access to education, healthcare, justice, and economic opportunity — measurable through comparable development indicators', 'Minority community leaders holding religious ceremonies publicly', 'Minority communities having more seats in Parliament than their population share'], ans: 1, exp: 'The best evidence of democracy delivering for minorities is substantive: equal outcomes in development indices — literacy, health, income, security. Political representation matters but true inclusion means equal life chances, not just symbolic representation or freedom to practise religion.' },
  { q: 'The phrase "democracy is not just an idea but a practice" means:', opts: ['Democracy only exists during election campaigns', 'Democratic values must be embedded in how institutions actually operate daily — in fairness, voice, and accountability — not just in constitutional documents', 'Democracies have more practical problems than other systems', 'Democracy means all citizens must be actively involved in government at all times'], ans: 1, exp: 'The NCERT text emphasises that democracy must go beyond formal constitutional provisions to be practised daily in how governments treat citizens, how institutions are accessible, how laws are enforced equally, and how power is exercised. Formal democracy without substantive practice is hollow.' },
  { q: 'Social conflicts in a democracy are considered manageable because:', opts: ['Democracy bans all protests and strikes', 'Democracy provides institutional channels — elections, courts, Parliament, free speech — for conflicts to be expressed and negotiated without turning violent', 'Democratic governments always satisfy all social groups', 'Social conflicts do not exist in developed democracies'], ans: 1, exp: 'A key strength of democracy is that it institutionalises conflict management through non-violent channels. Interest groups can lobby, parties can represent diverse voices, courts provide redress, and Parliament debates — all reducing the probability that unaddressed grievances explode into civil violence or revolution.' },
  { q: 'The "dignity of the poor" as an outcome of democracy is evidenced by:', opts: ['Free meals provided by all democratic governments', 'The formal legal equality of all citizens regardless of economic status — the poor can vote, contest elections, access courts, and be treated equally before the law', 'Economic prosperity achieved within two generations of democracy', 'Special tax exemptions for those below the poverty line'], ans: 1, exp: 'Democracy confers dignity on the poor through formal equal citizenship — one person, one vote regardless of wealth; equal access to courts; constitutional rights regardless of income. While poverty persists, democracy\'s formal equality is historically unprecedented — feudal and colonial systems had no such equality.' },
  { q: 'India\'s Comptroller and Auditor General (CAG) contributes to democratic outcomes by:', opts: ['Managing the national budget allocation', 'Independently auditing government expenditure and reporting to Parliament, enabling financial accountability', 'Advising the government on economic policy', 'Controlling the foreign exchange reserves'], ans: 1, exp: 'The CAG (Article 148) independently audits all accounts of the Union and state governments and reports to Parliament/state legislatures. This makes government spending transparent and accountable, a vital institutional mechanism for the democratic outcome of financial accountability.' },
  { q: 'Why does NCERT emphasise that "democracy cannot be judged by economic growth alone"?', opts: ['Because democracies never achieve economic growth', 'Because democracy\'s core value is not efficiency but equal dignity, rights, accountability, and peaceful governance — for which no growth figure can substitute', 'Because economists have disproved the democracy-growth link entirely', 'Because growth statistics are always manipulated by democratic governments'], ans: 1, exp: 'NCERT argues that reducing democracy to economic performance misses its fundamental purpose: to provide equal dignity, political rights, accountability, and peaceful governance to all citizens. A high-growth authoritarian state that suppresses rights is worse than a slower-growing democracy that protects dignity and participation.' },
  { q: 'Free and fair elections are considered a key indicator of democratic quality because they:', opts: ['Guarantee that the best-qualified candidate always wins', 'Allow citizens to hold rulers accountable by the threat and act of removal, incentivising responsive governance', 'Prevent all forms of political corruption', 'Ensure only honest candidates contest'], ans: 1, exp: 'Regular, free, and fair elections are the cornerstone of democratic accountability. The knowledge that they can be voted out incentivises governments to be responsive to citizens\' needs. Elections are not perfect, but no other mechanism gives citizens comparable power over their rulers.' },
  { q: 'The Election Commission of India\'s role in strengthening democratic outcomes includes:', opts: ['Writing laws that Parliament must pass', 'Conducting free and fair elections, implementing the Model Code of Conduct, and controlling election expenditure to ensure a level playing field', 'Appointing the Prime Minister', 'Adjudicating post-election disputes in the Supreme Court'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Election Commission — a constitutionally independent body (Article 324) — oversees free and fair elections, enforces the Model Code of Conduct, monitors election expenditure, and takes action against malpractices. Its credibility is central to the democratic outcomes of free expression of voter preference.' },
  { q: 'The "right to recall" elected representatives is sometimes demanded as an improvement to democratic accountability. It would mean:', opts: ['Representatives can voluntarily resign anytime', 'Voters can, mid-term, initiate a process to remove an elected representative who has failed to perform', 'Parliament can recall representatives from party positions', 'The Supreme Court can nullify election results'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Right to Recall (not yet implemented in India at the parliamentary/assembly level) would allow voters to petition for and vote to remove an elected representative between elections, enhancing accountability. Goa, Chhattisgarh, and some other states have limited forms of this for local bodies.' },
  { q: 'Which public survey instrument has been used to assess Indian citizens\' satisfaction with democracy?', opts: ['Doing Business Index (World Bank)', 'CSDS-Lokniti National Election Studies and Afrobarometer-like surveys', 'Transparency International Corruption Perceptions Index', 'Human Development Index (UNDP)'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Centre for the Study of Developing Societies (CSDS-Lokniti) conducts National Election Studies and other surveys that track Indian voters\' attitudes to democracy, satisfaction with government performance, trust in institutions, and political participation — providing empirical data on democratic outcomes in India.' },
  { q: 'The argument that "democracy is better at handling differences" refers to its ability to:', opts: ['Force all social groups to agree on every issue', 'Provide representation, negotiation, and legal protection so that diverse communities can coexist without violence', 'Homogenise cultural differences through majority rule', 'Make the judiciary the final arbiter of all social conflicts'], ans: 1, exp: 'Diverse societies — like India — contain many competing identities and interests. Democracy\'s multi-party system, federalism, reservations, and legal rights allow diverse groups to negotiate their differences through elections, coalitions, and law rather than violence, giving democracy an advantage over authoritarian uniformity.' },
  { q: 'The declining faith of citizens in political parties (as reported in surveys) is a concern for democratic health because:', opts: ['It shows citizens prefer monarchy', 'Political parties are the primary vehicle for aggregating citizen preferences into policy — weakening parties can mean weakening citizen influence over governance', 'Fewer parties means more effective governance', 'Party decline is always followed by military coups'], ans: 1, exp: 'Political parties translate citizen preferences into electoral choices and policy. When citizens lose faith in all parties, voter turnout falls, populism rises, and the link between public opinion and government policy weakens. Healthy democracy requires at least some degree of trust in political institutions including parties.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is a "procedural" outcome of democracy rather than a "substantive" outcome?', opts: ['Reducing poverty among tribal communities', 'Ensuring that elections are held regularly, freely, and fairly', 'Providing equal pay for women', 'Eliminating caste discrimination in daily life'], ans: 1, exp: 'Procedural outcomes of democracy relate to the proper functioning of democratic processes — free elections, rule of law, freedom of expression. Substantive outcomes relate to real-world results — reduced poverty, gender equality, minority rights. NCERT stresses both, arguing good procedures tend over time to generate better substantive outcomes.' },
  { q: 'India\'s parliamentary question hour is an example of which democratic outcome?', opts: ['Economic equity', 'Governmental accountability — ministers must answer to elected representatives and through them to citizens', 'Judicial independence', 'Federalism'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Question Hour in Parliament (first hour of each sitting) allows MPs to ask ministers questions about their ministries, forcing the government to explain and justify its actions on the floor of the house. This parliamentary accountability is a distinctly democratic mechanism of oversight.' },
  { q: 'The challenge of "elite capture" in democracy refers to:', opts: ['Elite athletes winning sports tournaments with government funding', 'When wealthy, upper-caste, or powerful groups disproportionately control the democratic process — elections, parties, media — marginalising the poor and minorities', 'Elite universities capturing government education funding', 'Bureaucratic elite dominating the elected government'], ans: 1, exp: 'Elite capture is a pervasive challenge in democratic systems where wealthy or powerful groups use money, connections, and social capital to dominate elections, parties, media, and policy-making, undermining the principle of political equality and reducing democracy\'s responsiveness to the poor majority.' },
  { q: 'Social movements like RTI movement, anti-corruption movement (India Against Corruption), and LGBTQ+ rights movements are best understood in the context of democratic outcomes as:', opts: ['Anti-democratic forces trying to bypass Parliament', 'Civil society using democratic freedoms (speech, assembly, petition) to demand better outcomes and expand the scope of rights', 'Government-sponsored advocacy campaigns', 'International NGO interference in Indian politics'], ans: 1, exp: 'Social movements in a democracy use constitutionally protected freedoms — expression, assembly, petition — to mobilise public opinion and pressure the government for better outcomes. They expand democracy from within, showing that democracy is not just elections but continuous civic engagement. RTI activism led directly to the RTI Act, 2005.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following would indicate a HIGH QUALITY of democratic outcomes in a country?', opts: ['The ruling party wins 95% of votes in every election', 'High voter turnout, low corruption index scores, high Human Development Index, strong press freedom, and low political violence', 'A strong military that controls internal security', 'No opposition parties to slow down governance'], ans: 1, exp: 'A high-quality democracy produces measurable outcomes: meaningful participation (voter turnout), accountability (low corruption), social welfare (HDI), freedom (press freedom index), and peaceful governance (low political violence). 95% vote shares for a single party suggest fraud, not quality democracy.' },
  { q: 'The NCERT text argues that challenges to democracy should be addressed through:', opts: ['Abandoning democracy for a more efficient authoritarian system', 'Deepening and expanding democratic institutions, rights, and participation rather than replacing democracy', 'Allowing military to temporarily take charge to clean up politics', 'Restricting voting to educated citizens only'], ans: 1, exp: 'NCERT\'s position is that the response to democracy\'s failures should be "more and better democracy" — strengthening institutions, increasing participation, enforcing accountability, and expanding rights — not replacing democracy with authoritarianism, which creates worse long-term outcomes.' },
  { q: 'The concept of "free press" as essential to democratic outcomes means:', opts: ['All newspapers must be free of charge', 'Media independent of government control that can investigate and report on government actions, exposing wrongdoing and informing the public', 'Only government-approved media can operate', 'The press must support the ruling party\'s policies'], ans: 1, exp: 'A free press — protected from government censorship and ownership control — acts as the "fourth estate" of democracy. By investigating corruption, exposing policy failures, and informing citizens, independent media creates the informed electorate and accountability pressure essential to democratic outcomes.' },
  { q: 'In the context of democratic outcomes, what does "rule of law" mean?', opts: ['Only lawyers can hold governmental positions', 'No individual, including the government, is above the law — all are equally subject to the laws of the land, enforced by an independent judiciary', 'Parliament can override Supreme Court judgements', 'The government makes all rules for citizens to follow unilaterally'], ans: 1, exp: 'Rule of law means that every person — including government ministers, police, and the powerful — is equally subject to the law, which is applied consistently and impartially by an independent judiciary. This prevents tyranny and arbitrary exercise of power, a foundational democratic outcome.' },
  { q: 'The "conflict resolution" advantage of democracy over dictatorship is best illustrated by:', opts: ['India\'s handling of diverse linguistic demands through linguistic reorganisation of states rather than violent suppression', 'India\'s military control of conflict zones', 'India\'s rapid economic growth resolving all conflicts', 'India\'s foreign policy preventing international conflicts'], ans: 0, exp: 'India\'s linguistic reorganisation of states (1956) is a classic example of democratic conflict resolution — a massive potential separatist movement across multiple linguistic groups was managed through negotiation, constitutional amendment, and political accommodation rather than violent suppression, which a dictatorship might have employed.' },
  { q: 'Why is low voter turnout among youth considered a warning sign for democratic outcomes?', opts: ['Youth not voting saves government money on elections', 'Young citizens not participating means future leaders are disconnected from electoral accountability, weakening democracy\'s long-term health', 'Youth low turnout improves election quality', 'Youth are adequately represented by older voters'], ans: 1, exp: 'Youth disengagement from voting weakens the democratic chain of accountability — politicians who don\'t fear young voters\' judgement have less incentive to address youth concerns (employment, climate, education). A democracy whose future citizens disengage from its processes loses its regenerative capacity.' },
  { q: 'The demand for "proportional representation" as an improvement to Indian elections relates to the concern that:', opts: ['Candidates receive too much election funding proportionally', 'Under the first-past-the-post system, parties can win large seat majorities with a minority of votes, reducing the overall representativeness of Parliament', 'Votes must be proportionate to the size of constituencies', 'Proportional voting is more resistant to booth capturing'], ans: 1, exp: 'Under India\'s first-past-the-post (FPTP) electoral system, a party can win a parliamentary majority with around 35–40% of votes. Proportional representation would ensure seat shares more closely match vote shares, making Parliament more representative of the full spectrum of citizen preferences — an improvement in democratic outcomes.' },
  { q: 'The ultimate test of democratic outcomes, according to the NCERT perspective, is:', opts: ['Whether the GDP doubles in a decade', 'Whether ordinary citizens — especially the poor, marginalised, and minority — feel that democracy has improved their lives and dignified their existence', 'Whether the ruling party wins successive elections', 'Whether India ranks high on global economic competitiveness indices'], ans: 1, exp: 'NCERT\'s final standard for evaluating democratic outcomes is whether democracy makes a real difference to the lives of ordinary and marginalised citizens — providing them dignity, voice, rights, and opportunity. Economic metrics matter, but the human-centred assessment of dignity and empowerment is democracy\'s deepest promise.' },
  { q: 'The "separation of powers" contributes to democratic outcomes by:', opts: ['Concentrating power in the executive for efficient governance', 'Distributing power among legislature, executive, and judiciary so each checks the others, preventing authoritarian abuse', 'Giving the judiciary supreme power over all branches', 'Ensuring Parliament controls the courts'], ans: 1, exp: 'Separation of powers — legislature makes laws, executive implements them, judiciary interprets them — creates mutual checks and balances. This structural design prevents any single branch from monopolising power, a key institutional guarantee of democratic outcomes including rule of law and rights protection.' },
  { q: 'Periodic elections are essential to democratic outcomes because they:', opts: ['Are expensive and waste national resources', 'Force rulers to seek renewed consent of the governed, making government continuously accountable to citizens', 'Allow only the educated to influence policy', 'Guarantee that the best possible government is always formed'], ans: 1, exp: 'Regular, periodic elections are the fundamental mechanism of democratic accountability. The requirement to seek fresh electoral mandates prevents rulers from becoming permanently unaccountable. Even if elections don\'t always produce ideal governments, they provide citizens the periodic power to change course.' },
  { q: 'Which institution in India is most directly responsible for ensuring free and fair elections — a core democratic outcome?', opts: ['Supreme Court of India', 'Election Commission of India', 'Parliament of India', 'Comptroller and Auditor General'], ans: 1, exp: 'The Election Commission of India (Article 324) is a constitutional body exclusively responsible for superintending, directing, and controlling elections. Its independence from the executive is crucial for ensuring elections are free, fair, and credible — the foundational democratic outcome from which all others flow.' },
  { q: 'India\'s democracy is sometimes criticised for "majoritarianism." This refers to:', opts: ['Majority party winning elections by large margins', 'When the majority community uses its numerical dominance to override minority rights, threatening democratic equality', 'Majority of voters abstaining from elections', 'Majority of laws being passed without debate'], ans: 1, exp: 'Majoritarianism is the tendency in democracies where the numerical majority (religious, ethnic, or linguistic) uses its electoral power to dominate and marginalise minorities, violating the democratic principle that equal rights must be guaranteed to all regardless of numbers. India\'s Constitution explicitly protects minority rights against this.' },
  { q: 'The model of "deliberative democracy" emphasises that democratic quality depends on:', opts: ['Maximum number of elections per year', 'Quality of public debate and reasoning — citizens genuinely discussing issues and government decisions based on evidence and argument', 'Only elected representatives deliberating in Parliament', 'Speed of law-making'], ans: 1, exp: 'Deliberative democracy argues that democracy\'s legitimacy and quality derive not just from voting but from the quality of public reasoning and discussion before decisions are made. A democracy where policies are debated with evidence, diverse voices, and transparency produces better outcomes than mere electoral majoritarianism.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following BEST explains why democracies are considered more stable in the long run compared to authoritarian regimes?', opts: ['Democratic governments never make mistakes', 'Democracies have institutional mechanisms (elections, courts, free press) to correct errors without systemic breakdown, while authoritarian systems accumulate uncorrected errors until collapse', 'Democracies have more military power', 'Economic growth is always higher in democracies ensuring stability'], ans: 1, exp: 'Democracies are self-correcting — elections remove failed governments, courts strike down unconstitutional acts, free media exposes wrongdoing. Authoritarian systems lack these feedback mechanisms, allowing errors to compound until the system collapses (as seen in the Soviet Union, Arab Spring countries). This self-correction makes democracy more resilient long-term.' },
  { q: 'India\'s democratic system is described as a "work in progress" because:', opts: ['India has not yet held enough elections', 'Formal democratic institutions are in place but substantive outcomes — equal rights, reduced poverty, minority protection — remain incompletely realised, requiring continuous reform', 'India does not have a written constitution', 'India\'s military has not yet been fully brought under civilian control'], ans: 1, exp: 'India is the world\'s largest democracy with robust institutions — universal franchise, independent judiciary, free press. But challenges persist: inequality, corruption, communalism, caste discrimination, and uneven development. Democracy must continually deepen its substantive achievements, not just its formal procedures — it remains a continuing project.' },
];
